\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Commutation and the Decline of Labour Services\\ in Thirteenth-Century England}
\author{H.~Pemberton}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The commutation of labour services into money payments was
long treated as a symptom of the commercialisation of the
English countryside in the decades after 1180.
Recent work on manorial accounts has shifted the debate from
grand chronology toward the bargaining position of tenants.

\section{Bargaining Over Services}
Customary tenants owed a mixture of week-work and boon-work
whose burden varied considerably from manor to manor.
The tenant could instead commute the obligation into a money payment known as "scutage", assessed in-lieu of service and recorded in the pipe rolls of the exchequer.
The spread of such arrangements was uneven:
commutation advanced fastest on the demesne-poor manors of the
east, where lords had greater need of cash than of labour.

\section{The Evidence of the Accounts}
Manorial accounts reveal the pace of commutation only
indirectly, since many lords continued to record services
as if they had been rendered in kind.
Where the account lists both the service owed and the payment
received, the historian can track the substitution year by year.

\section{Discussion}
Commutation should be read as a negotiation rather than a
secular drift: tenants purchased predictability, while lords
converted uncertain labour into revenue that could be spent
or lent elsewhere in the estate economy.

\section{Conclusion}
The evidence assembled here supports a bargaining account of
commutation and cautions against treating the decline of
labour services as an automatic consequence of monetisation.
\end{document}

